---
// /mesonet/wfs/: the OGC API - Features service, at the old path.
// Source: mco-website/_pages/mesonet/mesonet_wfs.md. Connection steps, code
// and specifications unchanged; the announcement opener is rewritten per
// docs/VOICE.md.
//
// The old page's live map is not migrated: it loaded MapLibre 3.6.2 and OSM
// tiles from unpinned CDNs, colored stations with a hand-picked ramp rather
// than MCO.palette, and captured page scroll (CLAUDE.md, HOUSE-STYLE §6, §7).
// The Mesonet Explorer is the live map; this page links it.
//
// The service URL stays on mesonet.climate.umt.edu, the name the service
// gives itself in its own links. Both it and mesonet2 answered 200 on
// 23 Oct 2026 (mesonet-gateway routes /api/latest/wfs* to the pygeoapi
// Lambda).
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { tool } from '../../lib/tools';
import { url } from '../../lib/paths';

const OGC_FEATURES = 'https://ogcapi.ogc.org/features/';
const SERVICE = 'https://mesonet.climate.umt.edu/api/latest/wfs';
const ITEMS = `${SERVICE}/collections/stations_latest/items`;
const explorer = tool('Mesonet Explorer');

const PYTHON = `import requests

# Fetch the stations with latest observations
url = "${ITEMS}"
params = {"limit": 100}

response = requests.get(url, params=params)
data = response.json()

for feature in data['features']:
    print(f"Station: {feature['id']}, Temp: {feature['properties']['Air Temperature @ 2 m [°F]']}")`;

const R = `library(sf)

# Load the Montana Mesonet data directly into an sf object
mesonet_url <- "${ITEMS}"
mesonet_data <- st_read(mesonet_url)

# View the first few rows
head(mesonet_data)`;
---
<Base short="Mesonet OGC API"
      description="Stream the latest Montana Mesonet observations into QGIS, ArcGIS, Python or R through an OGC API - Features service.">
  <!-- Copy drafted with AI assistance; needs MCO staff review before launch (UM AI disclosure). -->
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Montana Mesonet spatial services (OGC API)</h1>
    <p>
      We publish the Mesonet's latest observations as an{' '}
      <a href={OGC_FEATURES}>OGC API - Features</a> service, so GIS software and analysis tools can
      read them directly instead of working from downloaded files.
    </p>
    <p>
      The connection is live. Each time a map is refreshed or panned across the state, the
      software fetches the most recent observations, air temperature, precipitation and soil
      moisture among them.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a quick look at the same observations without GIS software, the{' '}
      <a href={explorer.href}>{explorer.name}</a> shows them on a map.
    </p>

    <h2>Service URL</h2>
    <p><a href={SERVICE}><code>{SERVICE}</code></a></p>

    <h2>Connect from GIS software</h2>
    <h3>QGIS</h3>
    <p>QGIS has the fullest support for the newer OGC API standards.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open the <strong>Layer</strong> menu, then <strong>Add Layer</strong>, then <strong>Add WFS Layer...</strong> (or press <kbd>Ctrl+L</kbd>).</li>
      <li>On the <strong>WFS / OGC API - Features</strong> tab, click <strong>New</strong>.</li>
      <li>Name the connection "Montana Mesonet", paste the service URL above into the <strong>URL</strong> field, and click <strong>OK</strong>.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Connect</strong>, select the <code>stations_latest</code> layer, and click <strong>Add</strong>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>ArcGIS Pro</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Go to the <strong>Insert</strong> tab on the ribbon.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Connections</strong>, then <strong>Server</strong>, then <strong>New OGC API Server</strong>.</li>
      <li>Paste the service URL into the <strong>Server URL</strong> box and click <strong>OK</strong>.</li>
      <li>In the <strong>Catalog</strong> pane, expand <strong>Servers</strong> and drag the <code>stations_latest</code> layer onto the map.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>ArcGIS Online</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Sign in and open a new or existing map in Map Viewer.</li>
      <li>Click the <strong>Add</strong> (+) button and choose <strong>OGC WFS Layer</strong>.</li>
      <li>Paste the service URL into the URL field.</li>
      <li>Once ArcGIS has found the features, click <strong>Add to Map</strong>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Python and R</h2>
    <p>
      The service returns GeoJSON, which Python and R can read straight into a data frame.
    </p>
    <h3>Python (Requests)</h3>
    <pre tabindex="0"><code>{PYTHON}</code></pre>
    <h3>R (sf package)</h3>
    <pre tabindex="0"><code>{R}</code></pre>

    <h2>Technical details</h2>
    <p>
      The service caches observations and refreshes them every 60 seconds. Coordinates are in
      WGS 84 (EPSG:4326).
    </p>
    <p>
      It filters by location on the server, so GIS software asks only for the stations inside the
      current map view. That keeps it quick, even on a phone connection.
    </p>

    <p class="method-note">
      The service carries only the latest observations. Past records, derived values and CSV
      files come from the <a href={url('mesonet/data/')}>Mesonet API</a>, and every element is listed
      on the <a href={url('mesonet/variables/')}>variables page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
